urbndv8@ucscb.ucsc.edu (Parenthetical) writes:


>Hmmm, I just had an interesting idea about limiting magic use.  Why not 
>have the use of spells drop your permanent INT occasionally - "the spell 
>strains your brain - you feel something snap" - or drop your permanent HP's
>- "the strain of casting that spell wracks your body" - but once you
>learned a spell it would be yours forever.  There might also be a SMALL 
>possibility of summoning a demon by accident.

>You should still have spell points, which would be your short-term
>limitation, but the game would keep you honest about reusing identify or
>whatever too often in this way, which would be your long-term limitation
>(preventing someone from sitting around regenerating mana and casting
>identify or polymorph over and over.)

>Whether using a spell affects your brain/body should depend on how powerful
>it is.  It could be a roll of the dice, or it could be something that 
>happens every time you've expended X number of magic points.  X should
>be rather high of course, should depend on your INT, and should have
>a certain range of variability, so players wouldn't sit around basing their
>play on it.

>Naturally, a spell would be less likely/less often to affect your brain or
>body if you already had a high INT.  If you were WIELDING a magic staff
>(a weapon) the bad effects that spell casting had would be cut also,
>perhaps by 1/2 or more.  The wizard would face a choice: fight or chant.

>I really like this idea because it is in keeping with the notion of wizardry
>as a hazardous yet rewarding profession.

I like the idea of a spell having a probability of reducing your INT and/or
HP, but how about extending it to all the characteristics.  And how about
instead of when you read a spellbook, the spellbook disappearing, have 
reading the spellbook cast the spell.  This fixes one other problem in
that spells are not only like wands, but WEIGHTLESS wands.
Added to this, keep the MP low, spell cost high, and MP regeneration rate
low.  Example:
Your wizard has 5 spellbooks, but they are so heavy he can only carry two
of them.  He picks up the polymorph and genocide spellbooks and leaves
behind a fireball, dig, and identify.
He goes down a few levels and then finds a shop.
Dumps the 2 books and goes up to get the others.
On the way back up, gets chased by something tough (no spellbooks, no spells
to fight with).
It follows him up the levels, he grabs the fireball book and blasts him
a couple times.
Goes back down to the shop.
MP still haven't regenerated enough to cast another spell.
Wait
Casts a couple fireballs at shopkeeper, killing him.  Casting spells
caused minor brain damage (INT -1).
Makes polypile.
Casts polymorph on pile.  Strain of spell causes spellbook to burst into flame.
Emporer Lich appears.  Cast genocide.  Not enough MP.  Get lucky and spell
succeeds, but... spellbook bursts into flames, massive shock to system
lose 1/2 HP, CON -1,stuff catches on fire, brain damage, etc.
(trying to cast an incredibly high level spell wo/ enough MP).

I think you get the idea.

